  <static seed="687" source="Library/Michigan/Chap18Sec3/Q15.pg">
    <statement><p>Use the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals to calculate
<m>\int_C\vec F\cdot d\vec r</m> exactly, if
<m>\vec F = x^{2/3}\,\vec i + e^{y/3}\,\vec j</m>, and <m>C</m> is the
quarter of the unit circle in the first quadrant, traced
counterclockwise from <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>(0,1)</m>.</p>
<p><m>\int_C\vec F\cdot d\vec r =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0.586837</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>\vec F = x^{2/3}\,\vec i + e^{y/3}\,\vec j =
    \nabla\left(\frac{3}{5} x^{5/3} + 3 e^{y/3}\right)</m>, we have
<me>\int_C(x^{2/3}\,\vec i+e^{y/3}\,\vec j)\cdot d\vec r =
    \frac{3}{5} x^{5/3} + 3 e^{y/3}\bigg|_{(1,0)}^{(0,1)} =
    3 e^{1/3} - \frac{3}{5} - 3 = 3 e^{1/3} - \frac{18}{5}.</me>



</solution>
